  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThis week into next weekend: Quiet and dry pattern, with clouds and \nthe occasional rain shower will be the norm through midweek. As \nthe upper low ejects northeast tomorrow, will open up into a \nweaker shortwave. There remains enough isentropic ascent for broad\ncloudiness through mid to late week (Wednesday to \nThursday).Temperatures moderate into the mid 70s to near 80 \ndegrees by midweek. Dry thermo profiles will climb as ascent and \nmoist advection increase. Precipitable water (PWs) climb around \nhalf inch to around 1.1 to 1.3 inches around mid-week (Wednesday \nto Thursday). This will lead to some isolated rain showers. In \naddition, a stronger shortwave will dive southeast of the Plains \ninto the Midwest, with a surface low and moving into the OH \nValley. This will drive a front southeast. All of this being said,\nthis could lead to some low chance (15 to 30 percent)/isolated \nrain coverage north of Interstate 20 into the Highway 82 corridor.\nUnfortunately, minimal rain totals, less than a tenth of an inch,\nwill make no dent in ongoing drought concerns and only continue to\nworsen again. \n\nThe synoptic pattern will become deeply amplified as another \nstrong spoke of energy on the western periphery of the mean \ntrough. This will drive reinforcing strong high pressure, around \n1025-1030mb, out of central Canada southward across the Great \nLakes to MS Valley into the Gulf Coast. Friday and likely again on\nSaturday, additional spokes of northern stream vorticity/shortwaves\nrotate our way down the westward side of the long wave trough \nwhich further deepens the upper troughing and reinforces the \nsuppressed, cool and dry weather. In fact, the pattern becomes so \namplified that at 500 mb, guidance generally agrees it will be \ncolder over portions of our area than in portions of far northern \nCanada with heights at that level sinking to 35 dam below \nclimatological average. Surface high will weaken slightly but this\nlooks to be the most efficient cold and another round of likely \nfrost and light freeze next weekend. Just prior, clear days and \nfull sun strong combined with stout pressure gradient will favor \ndeep mixing and potential increased wildfire danger around Friday.\nBlended guidance probs of light freeze are ow to medium chance \n(15 to 50 percent) and medium to high chance (60 to 80 percentage)\nnorth of Interstate 20, with low chance probs of falling below \n28F (10 to 30 percent). The growing season concerns will persist. \nThe exact depth and orientation of the cold airmass introduces \nuncertainty--our area will be on the western edge of the coldest \nairmass meaning small shifts east or west could mean multiple \ndegrees of difference in temperature. For that reason, will \ncontinue to monitor trends without formally issuing HWO products \nat this time.\n\nThe trend in temperatures for the week will the seasonable warmth\nthrough mid week (Wednesday to Thursday) to seasonably cool, \nhighs some 8 to 12F below (55F to 64F on Friday to Saturday) and \nlows some 12F to 15F below (28F to 34F Friday night and 30F to 38F\nSaturday night). Some patchy frost and light freeze is possible \nThursday night as well. /DC/86/",
